1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,040
Welcome to this new visual explanation.

2
00:00:03,040 --> 00:00:08,560
Today, we're diving into a truly fascinating economic and political analysis.

3
00:00:08,560 --> 00:00:12,800
We start with a fundamental question, explored by Daron Acemoglu,

4
00:00:12,800 --> 00:00:16,559
recently recognized globally, and his co-authors.

5
00:00:16,559 --> 00:00:20,600
Is support for democratic institutions a given,

6
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:26,160
or does democracy constantly have to prove its worth to survive?

7
00:00:26,160 --> 00:00:29,199
Through highly rigorous worldwide data,

8
00:00:29,199 --> 00:00:35,759
we will try to understand together what drives people to believe or stop believing in the democratic system.

9
00:00:35,759 --> 00:00:39,679
Okay, let's jump right into this empirical journey.

10
00:00:39,679 --> 00:00:43,039
We'll first explore the historical problem of democracy.

11
00:00:43,039 --> 00:00:46,320
We'll ask if experience actually creates support,

12
00:00:46,320 --> 00:00:49,480
and we'll discover the so-called success factor.

13
00:00:49,480 --> 00:00:53,159
We will then analyze growth, peace, and corruption.

14
00:00:53,159 --> 00:00:55,760
Before moving on to a direct comparison with autocracies,

15
00:00:55,759 --> 00:00:59,640
and finally, we will try to outline the future of our institutions.

16
00:00:59,640 --> 00:01:06,280
Let's move to the first section: the problem of democracy and its historical thesis.

17
00:01:06,280 --> 00:01:09,679
As is sadly known, we have recently witnessed a full 15 years

18
00:01:09,679 --> 00:01:13,239
of consecutive democratic backsliding at a global level.

19
00:01:13,239 --> 00:01:16,599
Therefore, a reflection naturally arises: in modern times,

20
00:01:16,599 --> 00:01:19,519
what prevents the collapse of the entire system?

21
00:01:19,519 --> 00:01:23,159
To understand this, let's go back for a moment to 1938.

22
00:01:23,200 --> 00:01:25,439
Justifying his historic New Deal,

23
00:01:25,439 --> 00:01:28,159
American President Franklin Delano Roosevelt,

24
00:01:28,159 --> 00:01:30,239
made a very astute observation.

25
00:01:30,239 --> 00:01:33,719
Democracy has disappeared in several other great nations,

26
00:01:33,719 --> 00:01:37,200
not because the peoples of those nations hated democracy,

27
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:41,319
but because they had grown tired of unemployment and insecurity.

28
00:01:41,319 --> 00:01:43,159
Roosevelt hit the nail on the head.

29
00:01:43,159 --> 00:01:45,799
The insight was that to support the system,

30
00:01:45,799 --> 00:01:50,719
citizens have a vital need for a positive and tangible experience with democracy.

31
00:01:50,760 --> 00:01:55,519
And this is precisely the central hypothesis of the research we are dissecting today.

32
00:01:55,519 --> 00:01:57,760
Which brings us to section two,

33
00:01:57,760 --> 00:02:00,039
experience creates support.

34
00:02:00,039 --> 00:02:01,200
Let's look at the data.

35
00:02:01,200 --> 00:02:06,960
The time has come to test this ancient and fascinating thesis on an unprecedented scale.

36
00:02:06,960 --> 00:02:11,039
And the numbers from this survey are simply extraordinary.

37
00:02:11,039 --> 00:02:14,039
The analysis is based on integrated value surveys

38
00:02:14,039 --> 00:02:16,960
and examines no less than 110 countries,

39
00:02:16,960 --> 00:02:19,919
with over 560,000 respondents.

40
00:02:19,920 --> 00:02:25,760
We're talking about a treasure trove of data meticulously collected between 1981 and 2018.

41
00:02:25,760 --> 00:02:27,680
But here's the real methodological gem.

42
00:02:27,680 --> 00:02:32,400
The researchers rigorously managed to isolate the impact of direct experience.

43
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,600
They have shown, in fact, that the level of democracy in a country,

44
00:02:35,600 --> 00:02:37,320
before a person's birth,

45
00:02:37,320 --> 00:02:41,360
has no significant impact on their future support for the system.

46
00:02:41,360 --> 00:02:45,840
In short, only personal, lived experience truly matters.

47
00:02:45,840 --> 00:02:48,400
And when these lived experiences are analyzed,

48
00:02:48,400 --> 00:02:51,120
a truly crystal-clear upward trend emerges.

49
00:02:51,120 --> 00:02:56,480
Empirical evidence shows that greater exposure to democracy has a stable and positive effect.

50
00:02:56,480 --> 00:03:00,159
To quantify, 10 more years of life in a democratic regime

51
00:03:00,159 --> 00:03:05,360
increase support for institutions by about 4% of its standard deviation.

52
00:03:05,360 --> 00:03:10,040
It's a very solid correlation, but beware, there's more beneath the surface.

53
00:03:10,040 --> 00:03:14,000
Thus, we enter the third section: the success factor.

54
00:03:14,000 --> 00:03:18,479
And here's the crucial revelation, the true plot twist of this study.

55
00:03:18,479 --> 00:03:25,759
Exposure to democracy generates support if and only if that specific democracy works.

56
00:03:25,759 --> 00:03:28,759
But what exactly does "work" mean?

57
00:03:28,759 --> 00:03:33,439
Acemoglu and his co-authors certainly weren't content with abstract definitions.

58
00:03:33,439 --> 00:03:36,199
They dissected the concept of success,

59
00:03:36,199 --> 00:03:39,159
measuring it through four precise pillars.

60
00:03:39,159 --> 00:03:42,159
First, the growth of the gross domestic product,

61
00:03:42,159 --> 00:04:00,759
second, rigorous control of corruption; third, the maintenance of peace and political stability; and fourth, public spending capable of providing real services to the community. Let's proceed to the fourth section, exploring the visual evidence of these first pillars: growth, peace, and corruption.

62
00:03:48,639 --> 00:03:53,400
and fourth, public spending capable of providing real services to the community.

63
00:03:53,400 --> 00:03:58,479
Let's proceed to the fourth section, exploring the visual evidence of these first pillars:

64
00:03:58,479 --> 00:04:00,759
growth, peace, and corruption.

65
00:04:00,759 --> 00:04:03,919
Let's see how they translate into real political support.

66
00:04:03,919 --> 00:04:07,479
Here, the data describes an unequivocal trajectory:

67
00:04:07,480 --> 00:04:12,000
when a democratic country goes through phases of strong and prolonged economic growth,

68
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:14,800
popular support literally takes off.

69
00:04:14,800 --> 00:04:18,160
Ten more years in an economically thriving democracy

70
00:04:18,160 --> 00:04:24,080
increase institutional support by a margin ranging from 4 to 8% of the standard deviation.

71
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:26,759
But what if democracy stagnates economically?

72
00:04:26,759 --> 00:04:31,319
Surprisingly, the increase in support is practically nonexistent.

73
00:04:31,319 --> 00:04:36,080
And this brilliantly illustrates another fundamental element: integrity.

74
00:04:36,120 --> 00:04:41,919
Living in a democracy that effectively controls corruption sends citizens' appreciation skyrocketing.

75
00:04:41,919 --> 00:04:46,800
Conversely, if governments are perceived as corrupt and unable to curb abuses,

76
00:04:46,800 --> 00:04:49,240
that positive effect vanishes into thin air.

77
00:04:49,240 --> 00:04:54,439
Disillusionment takes over as soon as the system is perceived as rigged.

78
00:04:54,439 --> 00:04:58,839
Peace and stability also play a critical role in this equation.

79
00:04:58,839 --> 00:05:02,159
The study provides a striking example: the Philippines.

80
00:05:02,200 --> 00:05:07,120
Despite having been classified as a democracy for much of the last 40 years,

81
00:05:07,120 --> 00:05:10,200
they have suffered from prolonged armed conflicts.

82
00:05:10,200 --> 00:05:14,200
The data shows that even in the presence of periods of economic growth,

83
00:05:14,200 --> 00:05:19,000
the lack of peace has prevented the consolidation of strong democratic support.

84
00:05:19,000 --> 00:05:22,600
Simply put, people prioritize security above all else.

85
00:05:22,600 --> 00:05:27,240
Concluding this analytical framework is the effect of public spending.

86
00:05:27,240 --> 00:05:30,000
People expect concrete services.

87
00:05:30,040 --> 00:05:33,720
A high level of public spending relative to the gross domestic product

88
00:05:33,720 --> 00:05:37,079
is strongly correlated with greater support for the system.

89
00:05:37,079 --> 00:05:43,680
In essence, institutions are defended and appreciated when they visibly invest in the well-being of everyone.

90
00:05:43,680 --> 00:05:47,319
We now come to the fifth section, a necessary step,

91
00:05:47,319 --> 00:05:52,759
the comparison with autocracies, which in this analysis serve as a perfect control group.

92
00:05:52,759 --> 00:05:53,920
One might ask,

93
00:05:53,920 --> 00:05:58,759
but what happens when an authoritarian regime delivers strong economic growth?

94
00:05:58,759 --> 00:06:02,199
Does economic success perhaps justify dictatorship?

95
00:06:02,199 --> 00:06:05,759
The contrast that emerges from the data is nothing short of surprising.

96
00:06:05,759 --> 00:06:09,439
As we have just seen, economic success under a democratic government

97
00:06:09,439 --> 00:06:12,399
enormously strengthens trust in those institutions.

98
00:06:12,399 --> 00:06:14,120
But a successful autocracy?

99
00:06:14,120 --> 00:06:15,279
Absolutely not.

100
00:06:15,279 --> 00:06:20,639
The data show no significant increase in appreciation for authoritarianism when the economy is doing well.

101
00:06:20,639 --> 00:06:25,159
This could happen because expectations of a regime are structurally lower,

102
00:06:25,160 --> 00:06:29,880
or perhaps because the lack of transparency and freedom prevents the creation of real civic trust,

103
00:06:29,880 --> 00:06:32,640
regardless of positive GDP figures.

104
00:06:32,640 --> 00:06:37,760
The researchers frame all of this through a very precise economic and psychological mechanism,

105
00:06:37,760 --> 00:06:40,360
defined as generalized learning.

106
00:06:40,360 --> 00:06:46,040
Essentially, it means that support for democracy is not a cultural trait etched in stone

107
00:06:46,040 --> 00:06:48,120
and it's not some kind of civic faith either;

108
00:06:48,120 --> 00:06:50,200
it's an entirely pragmatic update.

109
00:06:50,200 --> 00:06:54,879
There's a fantastic quote from the British poet John Betjeman cited in the study.

110
00:06:54,920 --> 00:07:00,360
"Our nation is founded on democracy and working drains," nothing could be truer.

111
00:07:00,360 --> 00:07:05,240
A genuine attachment to the system develops only when one directly experiences that it can provide

112
00:07:05,240 --> 00:07:08,560
tangible results useful for everyday life.

113
00:07:08,560 --> 00:07:14,719
And here we are at the sixth and final section, the future of institutions and the final conclusions.

114
00:07:14,719 --> 00:07:20,800
Connecting this immense volume of data to our present, the message that emerges is crystal clear:

115
00:07:20,840 --> 00:07:25,439
democracy feeds exclusively on its own tangible successes.

116
00:07:25,439 --> 00:07:29,840
This rigorous research imposes a deep and inescapable reflection.

117
00:07:29,840 --> 00:07:33,199
If global stability is not guaranteed by divine right,

118
00:07:33,199 --> 00:07:37,000
and if democracies avoid extremism and authoritarian collapse,

119
00:07:37,000 --> 00:07:40,240
only when they succeed in delivering growth, equity,

120
00:07:40,240 --> 00:07:45,560
and real services, how will we navigate the inevitable economic storms of the next decade?

121
00:07:45,560 --> 00:07:49,840
It's a fundamental challenge that goes far beyond pure academic theory

122
00:07:49,840 --> 00:07:53,640
and directly touches the practical foundations of our civil life.

123
00:07:53,640 --> 00:07:58,160
Thank you for joining us in this visual explanation, and until our next exploration.